now this map shows where infection rates  have been concentrated in England since  the start of the epidemic but it only  gives a partial picture because it  doesn't include people tested in the  community now public health England is  watching the increase in infections in a  number of other places but it's worth  stressing they haven't recorded anywhere  near the same numbers as Leicester and  these certainly are not lists of areas  facing imminent lockdown and if you look  at the data for Leicester you see why  the seven-day infection rate unless  they're using both hospital and  community testing is a hundred and  thirty five cases per 100,000 people  which is three times higher than the  next highest city the Labour leader  kiaus Tama has accused the government of  failing the city by not providing  testing data more quickly they had data  for pillar 1 Kovach tests that's NHS  care workers and testing hospitals they  didn't have pillar 2 tests the wider  test in the community now that may sound  technical but what that meant what that  meant was that the local authority  thought that what 80 positive tests in  the last fortnight when the real figure  was 944 the local authority was only  given the real figure last Thursday so  there was a lost week so it all boils  down to which figures are looked at  Leicester was identified using local  data from what's called pillar 1 mainly  Hospital testing and pillar 2 testing  done in the community so once you factor  in the latest figures based on both  pillars 1 &amp; 2 they show that Bradford  Barnsley and Rochdale came closest to  Leicester with 45 or more cases per  100,000 people but that's still quite a  long way behind left there so if we did  need to do a second plot down we could  we've also done it before but obviously  nobody wants that to happen we have  daily conversations with public health  England who are not have advising of a  second lockdown at the moment our cases  at least  going down day by day but they're still  higher than anybody would like and  therefore we need to redouble our  efforts and make sure they go down more  and to actually help people say safe now  at the moment public health England  doesn't publish daily figures from  community testing by local authority but  that's where three-quarters of  infections and now being identified  that's why this data will be critical to  spotting other hotspots and potential  local lockdowns
 

